# Inline code

@lede
Single backticks for code spans. Double backticks if the content itself
contains a backtick. That is the entire surface.
@end

## Single-backtick form

```brief
Use the `printf` function for that.
```

The content between the backticks is verbatim. No emphasis, no shortcodes,
no escape sequences run inside an inline code span.

## Including a literal backtick

To include a literal backtick inside inline code, switch to the
double-backtick form:

```brief
Run ``echo `whoami` `` to print the user.
```

The opening `` `` `` is followed by a leading space, the content (which may
contain single backticks), a trailing space, and the closing `` `` ``. The
leading and trailing spaces are stripped from the rendered output.

@callout(kind: note)
This is the *only* exception to Brief's "one canonical form" rule. It
exists because escape sequences cannot run inside a verbatim region —
there is no other way to embed a backtick.
@end

## What's *not* inline code

Indented four-space prefixes are not inline code spans, and not block code
either. Use a triple-backtick fence for blocks instead — the syntax is
covered in @ref[language/code-blocks.brf](Code blocks).

## In LLM mode

Inline code is emitted verbatim, with the surrounding backticks preserved.
A code span's content is never minified — minification rules apply only to
fenced code blocks with a recognized language tag.
